---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import commits from '../data/automat-commits.json'

const max = Math.max(...commits.map(c => c.c))
const BAR = 5, GAP = 1, H = 90, LABEL = 14
const width = commits.length * (BAR + GAP)
const bars = commits.map((c, i) => ({
  x: i * (BAR + GAP),
  h: Math.max(c.c > 0 ? 1 : 0, Math.round((c.c / max) * H)),
}))
---
<Base title="Projects" description="Things I build and maintain.">
  <p class="section-label">projects</p>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://trixbrix.eu">TrixBrix</a><span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      LEGO-compatible train tracks and accessories. I've been building the company
      and its manufacturing for years: a farm of ~150 3D printers, a webshop, and
      everything needed to turn filament into parcels.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2>Automat<span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      The internal software that runs TrixBrix. It started with one question: how do
      you plan printing when orders are made of products, products are made of parts,
      and every printer prints one part at a time? Then it grew. Today it handles:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>production planning — exploding orders into products into printable parts</li>
      <li>print farm scheduling: which part, on which printer, from which material, on which tray</li>
      <li>material and filament tracking, down to individual AMS slots</li>
      <li>failed prints and reprints, tracked across work shifts</li>
      <li>power management for the whole hall: smart outlets, paced power-on, auto-shutdown of idle printers</li>
      <li>warehouse stock, storage locations, and a recommender that suggests where to put things</li>
      <li>marketplace integrations, including Amazon FBA</li>
      <li>automatic repricing</li>
      <li>couriers, shipping labels, QR-based dispatch</li>
      <li>invoices and tax reports</li>
      <li>HR: shifts, absences, leave quotas</li>
      <li>a photo studio that triggers the cameras for product photos</li>
      <li>an internal knowledge base</li>
      <li>a Pip-Boy — the shop-floor terminal UI</li>
      <li>and everything in between</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart">
      <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${width} ${H + LABEL}`} role="img" aria-label="Monthly commits to Automat since 2017">
        {bars.map(b => (
          <rect class="bar" x={b.x} y={H - b.h} width={BAR} height={b.h} />
        ))}
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="chart-caption">
      3,913 commits, monthly, 2017 → today. Guess when AI agents showed up.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/ncr/omarchy-headphones">Omaphones</a><span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      Headphone controls in the Omarchy bar: per-earbud battery over Google Fast Pair,
      noise-cancelling modes over protocols I had to reverse engineer first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/ncr/rack-proxy">rack-proxy</a><span class="status">old</span></h2>
    <p>
      A small Ruby library I wrote years ago that accidentally became plumbing for
      much bigger projects.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">electronics</p>
  <div class="project">
    <ul>
      <li>an automatic SD card changer for 3D printers</li>
      <li>an old-school game console with an ESP32 as USB host, so it takes ordinary USB game controllers</li>
      <li>the electronics inside TrixBrix products</li>
      <li>a machine that places pixels</li>
      <li>a rocking cradle that rinses ABS-clogged printer nozzles in acetone</li>
      <li>a brick color sorter with a triple color sensor — it tells brick colors apart much better than I can</li>
      <li>a smartwatch that does exactly one thing: show telemetry from my Onewheel</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This list keeps growing.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
